course: dictionary
lesson: 11466
title: Advantageous: a helpful edge
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Advantageous is a formal word, so you will meet it in exams, reports and science books. It means helpful, and it gives you a better chance of success. Think of the word advantage: an advantageous thing is one that gives you an edge.
explain: «Having a map was advantageous when we got lost in the forest.» The map helped the children find their way, so it gave them a better chance. When you meet advantageous, ask: is this thing helping, and does it make success more likely? If yes, the word means helpful.
explain: Advantageous often goes with the word to. «Sleep is advantageous to your learning» means sleep helps your learning. Mutually advantageous is a useful phrase too: it means helpful to both sides, like a deal where both people win.
fact: Advantageous means helpful and giving you a better chance of success.
fact: Advantageous is a formal word, found in exams, reports and science.
fact: «An advantageous position» means a place where you have a helpful edge, such as high ground in a game.
fact: «An advantageous adaptation» is a body feature or behaviour that helps an animal survive, such as thick fur in the cold.
fact: A good check is to swap in helpful and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «Having a map was advantageous when we got lost in the forest.» What does advantageous mean? | *Helpful and giving a better chance | Very expensive | Dangerous and scary | Wet and muddy
exercise: Which word could replace advantageous in «A head start is advantageous in a race»? | Hungry | *Helpful | Heavy | Hidden
exercise: «Thick fur is an advantageous adaptation that helps a polar bear survive the cold.» What does this sentence tell you? | Polar bears live in hot deserts | *The fur helps the bear survive | The bear is cold and sad | Fur is always very long
test: Which phrase means helpful to both sides? | *Mutually advantageous | Very slow | Mutually angry | Once upon a time
test: «The new road was advantageous to the village, because shops could reach it easily.» What does advantageous to mean here? | Dangerous for | *Helpful for | Far away from | Unknown to
exam: Which sentence uses advantageous correctly? | *Having a head start was advantageous in the race. | I felt advantageous and then I cried. | The cake was advantageous to eat in the box. | The dog was advantageous under the bed.
